## How the modules behave<a id="module-behavior"></a>

All modules of the collection follow the same rules. The table gives each rule
in one line; the sections below add the details you need when a run surprises
you.

|      Topic       | Rule |
| ---------------- | ---- |
| Managed options  | A module changes only the options you set. |
{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-10 #}
| Finding a record | A record is found by its exact `name`; a changed name is another record. |
| Taking effect    | A resource module saves. Only `fixture_apply` activates. |
| Activation scope | An activation applies every pending change of its subsystem, also changes of others. |
| Lockout          | A change can cut off the API. The modules cannot undo it. |
{# end skeleton-variant #}
| Check mode       | Check mode reads and predicts; it never writes. |
| Failures         | A failed task says whether it changed anything. |
{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-11 #}
| Lost replies     | A module never sends a creation or an activation twice. |
{# end skeleton-variant #}
| Product versions | An untested product series warns; a known-bad build fails before writing. |

### What a module manages<a id="behavior-ownership"></a>

A module changes only the options you set in the task. An option you leave out,
or set to `null`, keeps whatever value the product has. To clear a field, set
it to an empty value, such as `""` or `[]`.

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-12 #}
`state: absent` deletes only the record you name. It fails while another record
still refers to it; delete or change the referring record first.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

Two writers on the same endpoint can overwrite each other. Let one play at a
time write to an installation. This includes inventory hosts that are aliases
of the same endpoint.

### How a module finds a record<a id="behavior-identity"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-13 #}
A module finds a record by its exact `name`. The product assigns the `uuid`;
you never pass it. Changing `name` in a task therefore does not rename a
record: it creates a second one.

A task fails when two records carry the name, or when the name belongs to a
record the product manages itself. A module treats a record as missing only
after it has read the complete list of records.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

### When a change takes effect<a id="behavior-activation"></a>

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-14 #}
A resource module saves a change; the product keeps running its previous
configuration. `fixture_apply` activates what is saved:

- `activate: auto` compares the saved and the running state of each record in
  `verify`, also of records you deleted. It activates when they differ and does
  nothing when they match.
- `activate: always` activates in any case. Without a `verify` list it cannot
  check the outcome and reports `verified: unverifiable`.

After an activation the module waits until the product runs the saved state of
the records in `verify`. The task fails when this does not happen within the
deadline.

An activation applies every pending change of the subsystem, whoever saved it.
The module checks only the records you name.

When the reply to an activation is lost, the module reads the running state
again. The task succeeds when it matches the saved state. Otherwise it fails
with `outcome: unknown`.

FIXME Keep the next paragraph only if a subsystem cannot report its running
state, and name each such subsystem, what its activation covers and how to
recover. Delete it otherwise.

Some subsystems cannot report what they run. Their apply modules send at most
one activation request per run and can only check that the service answers
again. They report `verified: unverifiable`, because an answering service does
not prove which configuration it loaded. A lost reply leaves the outcome
unknown, and the module does not send the request again.
{# end skeleton-variant #}

{# skeleton-variant: http-readme-15 #}
### Changes that can lock you out<a id="behavior-management-path"></a>

The configuration you manage includes the path the modules use to reach the
product. A saved mistake takes effect with the next activation and can end the
API access in the middle of a play. Recovery then needs the independent access
that [Requirements](#requirements) asks for.

`fixture_nested` refuses a rule that covers the address and port the API
listens on, also in check mode. This catches one kind of mistake and is no
guarantee: a proxy in front of the product or a change someone else saved can
still cut the path.

